1
00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:02.990
[S01] I am Julia Sumner Miller. I teach physics.

2
00:00:02.990 --> 00:00:05.110
[S01] Consider this astonishing thing.

3
00:00:05.110 --> 00:00:08.509
[S01] I have here a wheel resting on the tabletop,

4
00:00:08.509 --> 00:00:11.509
[S01] and on the bottom of the wheel, a spot.

5
00:00:11.509 --> 00:00:14.230
[S01] Now, when I roll the wheel in this fashion,

6
00:00:14.230 --> 00:00:17.350
[S01] the spot describes a very special curve.

7
00:00:17.350 --> 00:00:19.390
[S01] This curve is called a cycloid,

8
00:00:19.390 --> 00:00:21.949
[S01] and it has dramatic and astonishing properties,

9
00:00:21.949 --> 00:00:24.609
[S01] about which we shall shortly learn.

10
00:00:24.609 --> 00:00:27.609
[S01] What we must now ask is, why is it so?

11
00:00:48.979 --> 00:00:49.890
[S02] Hello there.

12
00:00:49.890 --> 00:00:51.509
[S02] Another program, Why Is It So?,

13
00:00:51.509 --> 00:00:53.990
[S02] with Professor Julia Sumner Miller.

14
00:00:53.990 --> 00:00:56.850
[S02] And again, some rather fascinating experiments.

15
00:00:56.850 --> 00:00:59.570
[S02] I think I must begin by asking again, why is it so?

16
00:00:59.570 --> 00:01:01.130
[S01] Isn't this, isn't this a beautiful thing,

17
00:01:01.130 --> 00:01:02.850
[S01] what this point does?

18
00:01:02.850 --> 00:01:05.409
[S01] Now, the beauty lies not alone

19
00:01:05.409 --> 00:01:08.010
[S01] in this rolling wheel and what the point does,

20
00:01:08.010 --> 00:01:11.450
[S01] but in the implication of the motion of this point.

21
00:01:11.450 --> 00:01:14.090
[S01] Let me show the people again what this point is doing.

22
00:01:14.090 --> 00:01:17.170
[S01] So if we can get a shot again, you see?

23
00:01:17.170 --> 00:01:18.290
[S01] See what the point is doing?

24
00:01:18.290 --> 00:01:19.450
[S01] It comes up to the top,

25
00:01:19.450 --> 00:01:21.329
[S01] and then it goes down to the cusp,

26
00:01:21.329 --> 00:01:23.810
[S01] and then it starts up and goes over again.

27
00:01:23.810 --> 00:01:26.129
[S01] Now, come to the board, and I will tell you the story.

28
00:01:27.569 --> 00:01:30.129
[S01] Here is the rolling wheel,

29
00:01:30.129 --> 00:01:32.890
[S01] and here is the surface on which it rolls,

30
00:01:32.890 --> 00:01:34.969
[S01] and here is the point, say, at the top,

31
00:01:34.969 --> 00:01:36.730
[S01] and this is what the point does.

32
00:01:38.010 --> 00:01:39.409
[S01] That's what the point does.

33
00:01:39.409 --> 00:01:41.849
[S01] Now, in itself, there isn't much to this,

34
00:01:41.849 --> 00:01:44.849
[S01] but it has a history which is absolutely enchanting,

35
00:01:44.849 --> 00:01:46.450
[S01] and I want to tell you the history.

36
00:01:48.250 --> 00:01:50.530
[S01] In the middle of the 17th century,

37
00:01:50.530 --> 00:01:53.530
[S01] there was a young Swiss by the name of Bernoulli.

38
00:01:54.769 --> 00:01:57.890
[S01] Indeed, he was one of a family of hundreds of Bernoullis,

39
00:01:57.890 --> 00:01:59.409
[S01] all of whom were geniuses.

40
00:02:00.290 --> 00:02:03.329
[S01] Now, this one, in the middle of the 17th century,

41
00:02:03.329 --> 00:02:04.530
[S01] raised a problem.

42
00:02:04.530 --> 00:02:05.930
[S01] Here's the problem.

43
00:02:05.930 --> 00:02:09.169
[S01] Given two points A and B

44
00:02:09.169 --> 00:02:11.050
[S01] in a vertical plane,

45
00:02:11.050 --> 00:02:12.409
[S01] but not in a vertical line,

46
00:02:12.409 --> 00:02:14.409
[S01] that is, I don't want B underneath A.

47
00:02:15.530 --> 00:02:18.610
[S01] Put a wire, let us say, between A and B,

48
00:02:18.610 --> 00:02:21.090
[S01] and let a bead slide on the wire

49
00:02:21.090 --> 00:02:22.409
[S01] in such a fashion

50
00:02:22.409 --> 00:02:24.729
[S01] that the bead gets from A to B

51
00:02:24.729 --> 00:02:27.009
[S01] in the least possible time.

52
00:02:27.009 --> 00:02:29.849
[S01] We call that the path of least time.

53
00:02:29.849 --> 00:02:32.969
[S01] Now, would not reason alone dictate an obvious answer?

54
00:02:32.969 --> 00:02:33.849
[S02] A straight line.

55
00:02:34.610 --> 00:02:35.610
[S01] So, right?

56
00:02:35.610 --> 00:02:37.530
[S01] So you put a bead on here and let it slide,

57
00:02:37.530 --> 00:02:38.689
[S01] and it gets from A to B

58
00:02:38.689 --> 00:02:40.930
[S01] in the briefest, shortest possible time?

59
00:02:40.930 --> 00:02:42.610
[S01] That is not right.

60
00:02:42.610 --> 00:02:44.569
[S01] The path from A to B

61
00:02:44.569 --> 00:02:46.849
[S01] of the shortest time is this.

62
00:02:47.969 --> 00:02:49.169
[S01] And do you see that that looks

63
00:02:49.169 --> 00:02:51.569
[S01] like a piece of that cycloid tipped over?

64
00:02:51.569 --> 00:02:54.009
[S02] It is, but Julius, this surely can't be right,

65
00:02:54.009 --> 00:02:55.569
[S02] because the distance is...

66
00:02:55.569 --> 00:02:56.370
[S01] This is so.

67
00:02:56.370 --> 00:02:58.009
[S01] This is the path of least distance,

68
00:02:58.009 --> 00:02:59.889
[S01] and this is the path of least time.

69
00:02:59.889 --> 00:03:02.129
[S01] Now, I'll tell you the history of this problem.

70
00:03:02.129 --> 00:03:04.449
[S01] Bernoulli posed it in the middle of the summer,

71
00:03:05.889 --> 00:03:08.449
[S01] and he gave until Christmas for a solution.

72
00:03:08.449 --> 00:03:11.250
[S01] See, half a year he gave the mathematicians.

73
00:03:11.250 --> 00:03:13.650
[S01] Promptly, he received a letter from Leibniz.

74
00:03:13.650 --> 00:03:15.330
[S01] Gottfried Wilhelm Leibniz?

75
00:03:16.729 --> 00:03:19.129
[S01] I have cut the knot of that beautiful problem.

76
00:03:20.449 --> 00:03:22.889
[S01] And then Leibniz suggested to Bernoulli,

77
00:03:22.889 --> 00:03:26.129
[S01] extend the time for a year from Christmas

78
00:03:26.129 --> 00:03:30.610
[S01] so as, so that the French and Italian mathematicians

79
00:03:30.610 --> 00:03:32.569
[S01] have no reason to complain

80
00:03:32.569 --> 00:03:34.250
[S01] of the shortness of the period.

81
00:03:34.250 --> 00:03:37.650
[S01] You see, Leibniz, with his German intellectual arrogance,

82
00:03:37.650 --> 00:03:41.090
[S01] felt that the French and Italian mathematicians were boys.

83
00:03:41.090 --> 00:03:43.810
[S01] So Bernoulli extended the problem for another year.

84
00:03:45.090 --> 00:03:47.169
[S01] Now, it came the turn of the New Year,

85
00:03:47.169 --> 00:03:50.330
[S01] and Bernoulli received a solution in the mail.

86
00:03:50.330 --> 00:03:52.090
[S01] No signature attached,

87
00:03:52.090 --> 00:03:54.650
[S01] but Bernoulli recognized the writing.

88
00:03:55.569 --> 00:03:57.210
[S01] He said in this way:

89
00:03:57.210 --> 00:04:00.409
[S01] "Tangwam ex ungue leonum."

90
00:04:00.409 --> 00:04:01.449
[S01] How's your Latin?

91
00:04:01.449 --> 00:04:02.370
[S02] Not very good.

92
00:04:02.370 --> 00:04:05.770
[S01] Just as the lion is known by its claw,

93
00:04:05.770 --> 00:04:08.449
[S01] the solution had come from Newton.

94
00:04:08.449 --> 00:04:09.449
[S01] Have we heard from Newton before?

95
00:05:10.750 --> 00:05:14.480
[S03] A brachistochrone.

96
00:05:15.689 --> 00:05:21.009
[S03] Now, if you had some Greek, I would tell you that brachistos means shortest

97
00:05:21.040 --> 00:05:23.180
[S03] and chronos means time.

98
00:05:23.579 --> 00:05:25.110
[S03] You see how beautiful the Greek is?

99
00:05:25.139 --> 00:05:29.709
[S03] That's why I have suggested the return of Latin and Greek to the public schools.

100
00:05:29.839 --> 00:05:30.949
[S03] See, brachistochrone.

101
00:05:31.350 --> 00:05:36.189
[S03] Now, this brachistochrone, which is this path here, has astonishing properties,

102
00:05:36.220 --> 00:05:37.850
[S03] more than I've yet narrated.

103
00:05:37.879 --> 00:05:38.790
[S03] And what are they?

104
00:05:39.089 --> 00:05:41.089
[S03] Let me draw the curve bigger still.

105
00:05:41.420 --> 00:05:43.060
[S03] A, B.

106
00:05:43.189 --> 00:05:43.930
[S03] Watch it.

107
00:05:44.230 --> 00:05:45.300
[S03] There's the curve.

108
00:05:46.160 --> 00:05:49.899
[S03] Now, if I put a bead here and let it slide on this wire,

109
00:05:50.199 --> 00:05:53.209
[S03] it gets to, it gets from A to B in a certain time.

110
00:05:53.740 --> 00:05:54.079
[S03] Huh?

111
00:05:54.110 --> 00:05:54.879
[S03] Certain time.

112
00:05:54.910 --> 00:05:59.579
[S03] Supposing I start the bead right here, instead of at A, supposing I started at C,

113
00:06:00.110 --> 00:06:03.379
[S03] it takes the same time to go from here as from here.

114
00:06:04.019 --> 00:06:05.889
[S03] Supposing I started at D,

115
00:06:06.420 --> 00:06:10.089
[S03] it takes the same time to go from here as from here, as from here.

116
00:06:11.189 --> 00:06:13.029
[S03] Isn't this beyond belief?

117
00:06:13.800 --> 00:06:14.899
[S04] It is, indeed.

118
00:06:15.230 --> 00:06:17.199
[S03] Indeed, let me start it right here.

119
00:06:17.230 --> 00:06:21.269
[S03] It takes the same time from here to here as from here to here, as from here to here,

120
00:06:21.300 --> 00:06:22.399
[S03] as from here to here.

121
00:06:22.899 --> 00:06:23.740
[S03] Is not that beyond?

122
00:06:23.769 --> 00:06:24.439
[S04] That is astonishing.

123
00:06:24.470 --> 00:06:25.410
[S03] Of course it is.

124
00:06:25.709 --> 00:06:26.579
[S03] Of course it is.

125
00:06:27.310 --> 00:06:31.449
[S03] And there lies the beauty of the point on the rolling wheel.

126
00:06:32.449 --> 00:06:35.720
[S03] Are you agreed that there is some drama in this history?

127
00:06:36.879 --> 00:06:40.990
[S04] And, and I know I probably risk a certain amount here by asking,

128
00:06:41.259 --> 00:06:42.220
[S04] how is this used?

129
00:06:42.250 --> 00:06:46.259
[S03] Oh, oh, the cycloid, the cycloid has astonishing properties

130
00:06:46.290 --> 00:06:51.100
[S03] in the making, say, of gears and how they mesh and, uh, lots of things.

131
00:06:51.129 --> 00:06:52.199
[S04] You don't really care, that's true.

132
00:06:52.230 --> 00:06:53.399
[S03] I don't care, I don't care.

133
00:06:53.639 --> 00:06:56.509
[S03] All I am interested in is the beauty and the drama

134
00:06:56.540 --> 00:06:59.009
[S03] and the elegance of this intellectual gymnastic,

135
00:06:59.180 --> 00:07:02.410
[S03] which Bernoulli gave rise to in The Problem.

136
00:07:02.439 --> 00:07:03.850
[S04] Hmm, yes.

137
00:07:03.879 --> 00:07:04.949
[S03] Isn't it, isn't it something?

138
00:07:05.420 --> 00:07:07.949
[S03] Do you see now, a little kid gets hold of this?

139
00:07:08.350 --> 00:07:12.589
[S03] And he needs to learn the mathematics to do this, which is at the highest flight.

140
00:07:12.819 --> 00:07:15.959
[S03] One needs what is called the calculus of variations.

141
00:07:15.990 --> 00:07:21.699
[S03] But I've spent enough on Bernoulli, on Leibniz, on Newton, on the brachistochrone,

142
00:07:21.730 --> 00:07:23.000
[S03] brachistos chronos.

143
00:07:23.029 --> 00:07:26.329
[S04] And the most fascinating story, but now what about these problems you gave to people

144
00:07:26.360 --> 00:07:27.699
[S04] at home to worry about?

145
00:07:27.730 --> 00:07:28.399
[S03] Last time, last time.

146
00:07:28.430 --> 00:07:29.769
[S04] The two eggs, one of them bad.

147
00:07:29.800 --> 00:07:30.139
[S03] Yeah.

148
00:07:30.439 --> 00:07:34.480
[S03] No, we have an assemblage of eggs in the refrigerator.

149
00:07:34.939 --> 00:07:37.949
[S03] And some we speculate are good and some we fear are bad.

150
00:07:38.279 --> 00:07:41.220
[S03] We wish to, uh, what shall we say?

151
00:07:41.250 --> 00:07:41.949
[S03] Distinguish them.

152
00:07:41.980 --> 00:07:42.350
[S04] Yes.

153
00:07:42.519 --> 00:07:46.420
[S03] So we put them in a great tank of water, in some float and some sink.

154
00:07:46.689 --> 00:07:48.790
[S03] You remember the little conundrum now?

155
00:07:48.819 --> 00:07:50.660
[S03] Which is it that sink and which float?

156
00:07:51.329 --> 00:07:53.829
[S03] You remember the old lady who threw them all out in despair?

157
00:07:53.860 --> 00:07:54.430
[S04] Yes.

158
00:07:55.500 --> 00:07:59.329
[S03] I should not really tell you this, but which ones do you think float?

159
00:07:59.829 --> 00:08:02.000
[S04] I think probably the bad ones.

160
00:08:02.029 --> 00:08:05.240
[S03] Oh, you have passed the course, again with some hope.

161
00:08:05.269 --> 00:08:06.410
[S03] The bad ones float.

162
00:08:06.439 --> 00:08:09.240
[S03] Now, why it is so, we do not explore.

163
00:08:09.269 --> 00:08:13.509
[S03] See, we're just giving the answer to make those who wish to know happy inside.

164
00:08:14.350 --> 00:08:18.189
[S03] I told you what Marcus Aurelius said, you know, at the turn of the Christian era.

165
00:08:18.889 --> 00:08:22.189
[S03] "All wish to know, but few the price will pay."

166
00:08:22.889 --> 00:08:24.889
[S03] This is the disease in the world.

167
00:08:25.790 --> 00:08:27.060
[S03] All right, so the eggs.

168
00:08:27.430 --> 00:08:28.629
[S03] Now, what happens first?

169
00:08:28.660 --> 00:08:29.699
[S03] You remember that one?

170
00:08:30.029 --> 00:08:35.139
[S03] We had a flask filled with water, which is at a certain level.

171
00:08:35.169 --> 00:08:37.139
[S03] Oh, I guess we'd better look at it here on the table.

172
00:08:37.169 --> 00:08:37.610
[S03] Okay?

173
00:08:37.639 --> 00:08:40.840
[S03] A round bottom flask, the water is at a certain level.

174
00:08:41.509 --> 00:08:42.879
[S03] And what did I ask you?

175
00:08:43.350 --> 00:08:47.379
[S03] I put this flask in a great vat of hot water, right?

176
00:08:47.919 --> 00:08:49.519
[S03] A great flask of hot water.

177
00:08:50.250 --> 00:08:53.120
[S03] And what does the water level in the tube do?

178
00:08:54.059 --> 00:08:58.389
[S03] Remember, I'm asking you, what is seen to happen first?

179
00:08:59.190 --> 00:09:04.230
[S03] And you whispered a solution last time, which I was emboldened to say was?

180
00:09:04.259 --> 00:09:04.629
[S04] Wrong.

181
00:09:04.659 --> 00:09:05.100
[S03] Yes.

182
00:10:08.169 --> 00:10:10.049
[S05] And then I blew two bubbles.

183
00:10:10.110 --> 00:10:12.909
[S05] I blew a small bubble and I blew a big bubble.

184
00:10:13.580 --> 00:10:16.179
[S05] And then I shut off the connection with the outside air

185
00:10:16.279 --> 00:10:18.419
[S05] so that the bubbles are connected with each other,

186
00:10:18.480 --> 00:10:20.019
[S05] but not with the outside air.

187
00:10:20.120 --> 00:10:22.350
[S05] Question, what now ensues?

188
00:10:22.450 --> 00:10:24.240
[S05] What do the bubbles do? What happens?

189
00:10:24.279 --> 00:10:26.009
[S06] I think my answer was that they equalize.

190
00:10:26.039 --> 00:10:26.539
[S05] Right.

191
00:10:26.610 --> 00:10:28.710
[S05] Everybody says they equalize.

192
00:10:29.039 --> 00:10:30.809
[S05] An astonishing thing takes place.

193
00:10:31.139 --> 00:10:32.450
[S05] They don't equalize.

194
00:10:33.320 --> 00:10:34.019
[S05] Watch.

195
00:10:34.350 --> 00:10:35.620
[S05] We will discover.

196
00:10:36.120 --> 00:10:37.990
[S05] Incredible, isn't it, Sanders?

197
00:10:38.429 --> 00:10:40.730
[S05] That the small one gets smaller.

198
00:10:41.029 --> 00:10:41.629
[S05] Come.

199
00:10:41.830 --> 00:10:44.029
[S05] Oh, this is really enchanting.

200
00:10:44.200 --> 00:10:44.830
[S05] Watch it.

201
00:10:45.570 --> 00:10:46.269
[S05] Watch.

202
00:10:51.409 --> 00:10:52.610
[S05] Do I have a small one here?

203
00:10:52.639 --> 00:10:53.240
[S06] Yes.

204
00:10:54.509 --> 00:10:55.679
[S05] And a big one there?

205
00:10:56.080 --> 00:10:57.610
[S05] Now let us watch them closely.

206
00:10:57.950 --> 00:11:00.080
[S05] And if there's... oh, oh, we had a little trouble.

207
00:11:00.149 --> 00:11:02.879
[S05] Some blow in the studio, I fear, is going to mess me up.

208
00:11:06.820 --> 00:11:07.720
[S05] Watch it now.

209
00:11:08.190 --> 00:11:09.590
[S05] I've shut off this tube.

210
00:11:09.889 --> 00:11:11.019
[S05] Watch the small one.

211
00:11:11.559 --> 00:11:12.860
[S05] Just watch the small one.

212
00:11:13.490 --> 00:11:15.529
[S05] We have a frightful blow in the studio,

213
00:11:15.600 --> 00:11:17.700
[S05] but do you see the small one getting smaller?

214
00:11:18.100 --> 00:11:19.029
[S05] Do you see it?

215
00:11:19.200 --> 00:11:20.570
[S05] Sanders, getting smaller.

216
00:11:20.769 --> 00:11:22.000
[S05] Tell me, do you see it?

217
00:11:22.029 --> 00:11:23.139
[S06] It is, it is shrinking.

218
00:11:23.169 --> 00:11:23.570
[S05] Good.

219
00:11:23.600 --> 00:11:25.370
[S05] And notice another fantastic thing.

220
00:11:25.440 --> 00:11:27.879
[S05] That the smaller it gets, the faster it gets smaller.

221
00:11:27.909 --> 00:11:29.509
[S05] Watch it, watch it now, watch it.

222
00:11:31.309 --> 00:11:32.909
[S05] Did you... I'll try it once more.

223
00:11:35.320 --> 00:11:36.620
[S05] Watch it now, watch it.

224
00:11:38.419 --> 00:11:40.190
[S05] Do you see it getting smaller, Sanders?

225
00:11:40.490 --> 00:11:43.019
[S05] And notice, the smaller it gets, the faster it gets smaller.

226
00:11:43.759 --> 00:11:44.490
[S05] Isn't that fantastic?

227
00:11:44.519 --> 00:11:45.759
[S06] Presumably, this one is getting bigger.

228
00:11:45.789 --> 00:11:47.529
[S05] Yes, but it's so large already

229
00:11:47.559 --> 00:11:49.299
[S05] that any change in it is hard to see.

230
00:11:49.330 --> 00:11:49.730
[S06] Yes.

231
00:11:49.759 --> 00:11:51.230
[S05] The calculus would tell you that.

232
00:11:51.370 --> 00:11:53.669
[S05] But I want to do it once more for another reason.

233
00:11:55.370 --> 00:11:57.169
[S05] What do I wish to point out here?

234
00:11:57.269 --> 00:12:00.440
[S05] I want you to make the observation that I would point out myself.

235
00:12:06.480 --> 00:12:09.450
[S05] What are you observing that should be observed?

236
00:12:10.480 --> 00:12:12.519
[S06] The only thing I can think of is the colors of bubbles.

237
00:12:12.549 --> 00:12:14.450
[S05] Now you have come to it, young man.

238
00:12:14.519 --> 00:12:16.120
[S05] Another hope to pass the course.

239
00:12:16.419 --> 00:12:18.620
[S05] Notice the pretty colors in thin films.

240
00:12:18.990 --> 00:12:20.759
[S05] We have some blow in the studio.

241
00:12:21.090 --> 00:12:21.960
[S05] You remember?

242
00:12:22.460 --> 00:12:25.029
[S05] Newton first observed these colors in white light.

243
00:12:25.299 --> 00:12:26.299
[S05] You know what he said?

244
00:12:27.269 --> 00:12:29.240
[S05] "In the year 1665,

245
00:12:29.269 --> 00:12:32.110
[S05] I procured me a triangular glass prism

246
00:12:32.240 --> 00:12:35.740
[S05] with which to try the celebrated phenomena of colors."

247
00:12:36.039 --> 00:12:40.480
[S05] So he cut now a slot in his window shut,

248
00:12:40.549 --> 00:12:42.519
[S05] you know, and he let the sunlight in,

249
00:12:42.549 --> 00:12:44.480
[S05] and then he saw on yonder wall

250
00:12:44.549 --> 00:12:47.620
[S05] red, orange, yellow, green, blue, and violet.

251
00:12:48.049 --> 00:12:49.720
[S05] And you know how he appraised that?

252
00:12:50.659 --> 00:12:54.259
[S05] "The most remarkable observation in all of his lifetime."

253
00:12:54.929 --> 00:12:56.360
[S05] And regarding nature...

254
00:12:57.029 --> 00:12:57.960
[S05] What did he say?

255
00:12:59.070 --> 00:13:02.039
[S05] "The oddest, if not the most profound,

256
00:13:02.139 --> 00:13:04.570
[S05] in all the operations of nature."

257
00:13:04.809 --> 00:13:06.470
[S05] Imagine looking at white light.

258
00:13:06.610 --> 00:13:08.340
[S05] Red, orange, yellow, green, blue, and violet.

259
00:13:08.379 --> 00:13:09.440
[S05] Isn't that staggering?

260
00:13:09.639 --> 00:13:11.549
[S05] So, the double bubble paradox.

261
00:13:11.779 --> 00:13:14.210
[S05] Now, free fall and horizontal projection.

262
00:13:14.450 --> 00:13:16.620
[S05] Oh, this one is enchanting too.

263
00:13:16.750 --> 00:13:18.220
[S05] You remember what we did?

264
00:13:18.289 --> 00:13:19.720
[S05] We had an apparatus.

265
00:13:19.889 --> 00:13:20.720
[S06] Two ping pong balls.

266
00:13:20.750 --> 00:13:21.720
[S05] Two ping pong balls.

267
00:13:21.789 --> 00:13:24.259
[S05] One was released to fall so,

268
00:13:24.320 --> 00:13:27.360
[S05] and the other was projected so as to take that path, right?

269
00:13:27.389 --> 00:13:27.889
[S06] Right.

270
00:13:28.460 --> 00:13:30.730
[S05] And you are of the view

271
00:13:30.759 --> 00:13:33.629
[S05] that why, this is a longer way to go and should take longer.

272
00:13:34.070 --> 00:13:35.639
[S05] But Galileo disposed of that,

273
00:13:35.669 --> 00:13:38.409
[S05] so you see, you should have been a student of Galileo's

274
00:13:38.440 --> 00:13:39.740
[S05] and done better with the problem.

275
00:13:40.480 --> 00:13:41.809
[S05] Why is it so?

276
00:13:42.009 --> 00:13:44.379
[S05] Well, I'll simply suggest the line of thinking

277
00:13:44.409 --> 00:13:47.210
[S05] and let the viewers handle it for themselves.

278
00:15:08.000 --> 00:15:10.549
[S07] My view would be that the holes would shrink.

279
00:15:10.580 --> 00:15:12.549
[S08] Shrink, that is, close up, get smaller.

280
00:15:13.580 --> 00:15:13.940
[S08] Yeah.

281
00:15:13.970 --> 00:15:14.269
[S07] Yes.

282
00:15:15.639 --> 00:15:19.070
[S08] Notice, all the virtue you demonstrated earlier is now taken away.

283
00:15:19.100 --> 00:15:19.580
[S07] Is gone.

284
00:15:20.009 --> 00:15:21.049
[S08] The holes get bigger.

285
00:15:21.809 --> 00:15:23.710
[S08] Now, let me give you an argument to help you.

286
00:15:24.509 --> 00:15:28.279
[S08] Do you remember this ring, which does not allow the ball to pass?

287
00:15:29.450 --> 00:15:32.750
[S08] And then we agree that if we heat the ring, the ball then does pass?

288
00:15:32.779 --> 00:15:33.210
[S07] Yes.

289
00:15:34.120 --> 00:15:36.149
[S08] As a point of departure for your thinking,

290
00:15:36.549 --> 00:15:40.379
[S08] this ring is nothing but a plate with a big hole.

291
00:15:40.889 --> 00:15:43.080
[S08] I would say it's a plate mostly hole.

292
00:15:43.789 --> 00:15:48.850
[S08] And holes in metal plates behave exactly as they would

293
00:15:48.879 --> 00:15:52.080
[S08] if they were not holes, but filled with the stuff which is not there.

294
00:15:52.110 --> 00:15:52.940
[S08] How do you like that?

295
00:15:53.409 --> 00:15:54.480
[S07] That's very bold.

296
00:15:54.610 --> 00:15:55.549
[S08] It's a good sentence.

297
00:15:55.809 --> 00:15:59.450
[S08] The hole gets bigger, wouldn't the plate get bigger if there was no hole in it?

298
00:16:00.149 --> 00:16:02.080
[S08] So this is a plate with a big hole.

299
00:16:02.110 --> 00:16:04.409
[S08] This happens to be a plate with a small hole.

300
00:16:04.679 --> 00:16:10.379
[S08] And generically then, all holes in plates get bigger when the plates are heated.

301
00:16:12.580 --> 00:16:14.049
[S07] I'm wrong too often, I assume.

302
00:16:14.080 --> 00:16:15.610
[S08] All right, well, do you see?

303
00:16:15.879 --> 00:16:20.549
[S08] Are not these old-fashioned, primitive things quite enchanting to engage in?

304
00:16:20.580 --> 00:16:21.049
[S07] Yeah.

305
00:16:21.080 --> 00:16:21.549
[S08] Sure.

306
00:16:21.779 --> 00:16:22.710
[S08] Now, where do we go?

307
00:16:23.480 --> 00:16:23.909
[S07] Well.

308
00:16:23.940 --> 00:16:27.610
[S08] Oh, yes, the, the, the beauty of the pendula.

309
00:16:27.980 --> 00:16:29.409
[S08] The beauty of the pendula.

310
00:16:29.649 --> 00:16:34.149
[S08] Here again, some motion explored by Galileo.

311
00:16:35.250 --> 00:16:36.909
[S08] I want you to see what we have here.

312
00:16:37.379 --> 00:16:37.980
[S08] Do you see?

313
00:16:38.850 --> 00:16:41.710
[S08] I have three simple pendula.

314
00:16:42.450 --> 00:16:43.909
[S08] Singular pendulum.

315
00:16:44.450 --> 00:16:50.779
[S08] Uh, and this, uh, case, uh, this, uh, this calls for A in the plural.

316
00:16:51.409 --> 00:16:52.480
[S08] Three pendula.

317
00:16:53.480 --> 00:16:54.710
[S08] Notice, a short one,

318
00:16:55.779 --> 00:16:56.649
[S08] a longer one,

319
00:16:57.850 --> 00:16:59.149
[S08] and a longer one still.

320
00:16:59.179 --> 00:17:00.250
[S07] Do you want to put this up on the, uh?

321
00:17:00.279 --> 00:17:01.980
[S08] Oh, put this up here, so, good.

322
00:17:02.409 --> 00:17:02.850
[S08] Yes.

323
00:17:03.149 --> 00:17:06.980
[S08] Some of these requirements for the camera escaped my mind.

324
00:17:07.309 --> 00:17:08.880
[S08] And that's what you are here for, to remind me.

325
00:17:08.910 --> 00:17:11.079
[S08] You know, I'm old and senile and forget quickly.

326
00:17:11.609 --> 00:17:13.779
[S08] So, what am I going to do with these?

327
00:17:14.309 --> 00:17:19.049
[S08] I am going to make an adventure that should make us feel jointly

328
00:17:19.369 --> 00:17:23.049
[S08] as Kepler felt after twenty-six years of labor

329
00:17:23.079 --> 00:17:26.980
[S08] in looking at the pages of data which Tycho Brahe had gathered.

330
00:17:27.309 --> 00:17:28.309
[S08] You know that story?

331
00:17:28.549 --> 00:17:31.779
[S08] Copernicus, Copernicus and his manuscript

332
00:17:32.150 --> 00:17:34.710
[S08] De Revolutionibus Orbium Celestium,

333
00:17:35.180 --> 00:17:38.410
[S08] which revolutionized the thinking about the world.

334
00:17:38.809 --> 00:17:42.369
[S08] And then the data which Tycho Brahe gathered over thirty years,

335
00:17:42.609 --> 00:17:46.579
[S08] with which he could do nothing because he was a very good observer, but a poor mathematician.

336
00:17:47.049 --> 00:17:51.609
[S08] And along came Kepler, who spent twenty-six years looking at ten thousand pages of numbers,

337
00:17:51.849 --> 00:17:56.710
[S08] and from it got Kepler's laws of motion with which the world goes around.

338
00:17:56.980 --> 00:17:58.410
[S08] So I'm going to be with Kepler.

339
00:17:58.440 --> 00:17:59.049
[S08] Watch me.

340
00:17:59.180 --> 00:18:00.109
[S08] Come, come here.

341
00:18:01.279 --> 00:18:02.849
[S08] Here is the support.

342
00:18:03.740 --> 00:18:05.369
[S08] And here is a pendulum.

343
00:18:05.849 --> 00:18:07.710
[S08] And here is another pendulum.

344
00:18:07.980 --> 00:18:09.710
[S08] And here is another one.

345
00:18:11.009 --> 00:18:13.980
[S08] And I have made this one ten centimeters long.

346
00:18:14.410 --> 00:18:17.049
[S08] And I have made this one forty centimeters long.

347
00:18:17.210 --> 00:18:19.710
[S08] And I have made this one ninety centimeters long.

348
00:18:19.809 --> 00:18:21.740
[S08] Notice the numbers, ten, forty, ninety.

349
00:18:22.279 --> 00:18:24.369
[S08] Now I start this one swinging.

350
00:18:25.250 --> 00:18:27.809
[S08] And I clock so many swings.

351
00:18:28.049 --> 00:18:32.109
[S08] Supposing with a stop clock, I count the time required for twenty swings.

352
00:18:32.140 --> 00:18:32.579
[S07] Good.

353
00:18:32.849 --> 00:18:38.480
[S08] The length of the bobs are ten, forty, and ninety centimeters, right?

354
00:18:38.509 --> 00:18:38.849
[S07] Right.

355
00:18:39.410 --> 00:18:43.450
[S08] Now, the time, I will say, for twenty vibrations,

356
00:18:43.650 --> 00:18:44.549
[S08] T sub twenty.

357
00:18:44.740 --> 00:18:48.250
[S08] I could count fifteen, or I could count forty-one, doesn't matter.

358
00:18:48.940 --> 00:18:51.940
[S08] The time for twenty vibrations of this one,

359
00:18:52.279 --> 00:18:54.940
[S08] in my, uh, laboratory,

360
00:18:55.049 --> 00:18:56.579
[S08] now, I don't know how I said that.

361
00:18:57.150 --> 00:18:59.849
[S08] In, in my home, I say, laboratory.

362
00:19:00.509 --> 00:19:02.369
[S08] And I tell my students, look,

363
00:19:03.309 --> 00:19:05.779
[S08] much labor and little oratory.

364
00:19:06.109 --> 00:19:06.980
[S08] Do you like that?

365
00:19:07.009 --> 00:19:07.369
[S08] See?

366
00:19:07.440 --> 00:19:07.849
[S08] All right.

367
00:19:07.880 --> 00:19:12.480
[S08] Now, the time for twenty vibrations is as follows, in my home.

368
00:20:06.430 --> 00:20:09.529
[S09] Which for one oscillation we call the period,

369
00:20:10.140 --> 00:20:13.920
[S09] is proportional to the square root of the length.

370
00:20:14.470 --> 00:20:18.950
[S09] And here we have uncovered, in a beautiful Keplerian fashion,

371
00:20:19.440 --> 00:20:22.369
[S09] how the period of a pendulum is governed by its length.

372
00:20:22.890 --> 00:20:24.519
[S09] Now, there's some more stuff in there.

373
00:20:24.660 --> 00:20:27.619
[S09] When the mathematics is done, it comes out like this.

374
00:20:28.869 --> 00:20:30.789
[S09] And for those who know a little physics,

375
00:20:30.890 --> 00:20:35.720
[S09] I remind them that this and this comes out of mathematics.

376
00:20:36.450 --> 00:20:39.420
[S09] So, what is my suggestion?

377
00:20:40.569 --> 00:20:44.509
[S09] I put this on the board, and I put this on the board, and I let it stand.

378
00:20:45.150 --> 00:20:48.740
[S09] And some little fellow, who has the hope of Faraday,

379
00:20:49.000 --> 00:20:50.920
[S09] suddenly sees the light of this,

380
00:20:51.049 --> 00:20:54.650
[S09] and he certainly must feel, as Kepler felt after 26 years,

381
00:20:54.759 --> 00:20:58.250
[S09] uncovering the mystery of the behaviour of the world.

382
00:20:58.990 --> 00:20:59.970
[S09] One more remark.

383
00:21:00.019 --> 00:21:02.170
[S09] Suppose I took any other length at random.

384
00:21:02.819 --> 00:21:04.349
[S09] See, 10, 40, 90 centimeters.

385
00:21:04.390 --> 00:21:08.650
[S09] Suppose I took 176 centimeters, or 1132 centimeters.

386
00:21:09.309 --> 00:21:11.059
[S09] Would not the time here

387
00:21:11.670 --> 00:21:15.839
[S09] bear the same ratio to that number as this does to that, and so on?

388
00:21:15.869 --> 00:21:16.259
[S10] Yes.

389
00:21:16.309 --> 00:21:19.059
[S09] Which I invite a young observer to pursue.

390
00:21:19.109 --> 00:21:20.059
[S09] Isn't this beautiful?

391
00:21:20.089 --> 00:21:20.740
[S10] This is fascinating.

392
00:21:20.769 --> 00:21:22.390
[S09] Sure, it is fascinating!

393
00:21:22.420 --> 00:21:25.450
[S09] This is the word that we must have always: dramatic.

394
00:21:27.390 --> 00:21:27.920
[S09] All right?

395
00:21:28.990 --> 00:21:29.849
[S10] Where are we going now?

396
00:21:30.190 --> 00:21:32.319
[S09] We are going now to explore

397
00:21:33.250 --> 00:21:35.720
[S09] why a lariat works.

398
00:21:36.269 --> 00:21:37.319
[S09] You know, you...

399
00:21:37.349 --> 00:21:39.119
[S10] Oh, yes, the cowboys, the Wild West.

400
00:21:39.150 --> 00:21:40.720
[S09] Do you have it here in Australia?

401
00:21:40.750 --> 00:21:42.450
[S10] Not used very much, except trick.

402
00:21:42.549 --> 00:21:44.920
[S09] Oh, no. Well, all right. Why do they work?

403
00:21:45.119 --> 00:21:46.849
[S09] Well, I'm going to show you why they work.

404
00:21:48.089 --> 00:21:49.119
[S09] I have a disc,

405
00:21:49.950 --> 00:21:51.769
[S09] mounted with a, fixed with a string,

406
00:21:51.990 --> 00:21:54.450
[S09] and a little screw eye here.

407
00:21:54.990 --> 00:21:56.849
[S09] And I'm going to fix this screw eye.

408
00:21:57.019 --> 00:21:58.950
[S09] Hold that in there, will you, for me? Thank you.

409
00:21:59.049 --> 00:22:01.519
[S09] I'm going to fix that into the chuck of this rotator.

410
00:22:02.250 --> 00:22:02.950
[S09] Uh-oh.

411
00:22:03.950 --> 00:22:05.049
[S09] Uh-oh, get there.

412
00:22:05.619 --> 00:22:06.769
[S09] That, thank you.

413
00:22:06.890 --> 00:22:08.569
[S09] I'm going to get it in there.

414
00:22:08.650 --> 00:22:10.049
[S09] And now, what am I going to do?

415
00:22:10.950 --> 00:22:13.250
[S09] Is it not really some mystery here?

416
00:22:13.289 --> 00:22:15.950
[S09] How am I going to show how a lariat works with this rig?

417
00:22:16.119 --> 00:22:17.950
[S09] But do you see how primitive my apparatus?

418
00:22:18.119 --> 00:22:19.450
[S09] Now, watch what I'm going to do.

419
00:22:19.789 --> 00:22:21.569
[S09] I'm going to turn the crank of this

420
00:22:22.250 --> 00:22:23.990
[S09] of this egg-beater drill.

421
00:22:24.920 --> 00:22:26.250
[S09] And this thing is turning

422
00:22:26.390 --> 00:22:29.690
[S09] about an axis for which we say the moment of inertia is so much.

423
00:22:29.789 --> 00:22:31.519
[S09] And now, watch what the disc does.

424
00:22:31.619 --> 00:22:33.049
[S09] What do you think the disc will do?

425
00:22:33.650 --> 00:22:34.690
[S09] What do you think it'll do?

426
00:22:34.720 --> 00:22:36.049
[S10] It seems to be speeding up.

427
00:22:36.119 --> 00:22:36.769
[S09] Yes.

428
00:22:38.220 --> 00:22:39.849
[S09] Now, we're having a little trouble.

429
00:22:42.119 --> 00:22:43.289
[S09] I'm having a little trouble.

430
00:22:43.390 --> 00:22:44.450
[S09] Now, do you see?

431
00:22:46.849 --> 00:22:47.990
[S09] There we are.

432
00:22:48.119 --> 00:22:49.119
[S09] I had a little trouble.

433
00:22:49.150 --> 00:22:50.390
[S09] What happened to the disc?

434
00:22:51.220 --> 00:22:54.170
[S09] Did it not come up to rotation in a horizontal plane?

435
00:22:54.220 --> 00:22:56.170
[S10] Could I use an American expression that flipped its lid?

436
00:22:56.190 --> 00:22:57.519
[S09] Yes, yes, yes, very good.

437
00:22:57.549 --> 00:22:58.170
[S09] Do you see?

438
00:22:58.490 --> 00:23:01.319
[S09] Now, you're going to say, Julius, how does that throw any light

439
00:23:01.390 --> 00:23:03.769
[S09] on how a lariat works?

440
00:23:03.920 --> 00:23:05.319
[S09] Well, a word about it.

441
00:23:06.349 --> 00:23:07.990
[S09] The rotation about this axis,

442
00:23:08.650 --> 00:23:10.950
[S09] this axis has a certain moment of inertia.

443
00:23:11.019 --> 00:23:12.450
[S09] This disc has for this axis.

444
00:23:12.519 --> 00:23:15.349
[S09] Now, when it comes up like that, it has a different moment of inertia.

445
00:23:15.819 --> 00:23:18.890
[S09] And we uncover a beautiful theorem in mechanics which says

446
00:23:19.119 --> 00:23:21.049
[S09] that a system in stable rotation

447
00:23:21.170 --> 00:23:23.450
[S09] tends to rotation about an axis

448
00:23:23.490 --> 00:23:25.890
[S09] for which the moment of inertia is a maximum.

449
00:23:25.950 --> 00:23:27.170
[S09] That's a pretty heavy sentence.

450
00:23:27.250 --> 00:23:29.720
[S09] Let me do it, however, with the chain.

451
00:23:29.750 --> 00:23:30.990
[S09] How much time do we have?

452
00:23:31.049 --> 00:23:32.349
[S10] Oh, about eight minutes or so.

453
00:23:32.390 --> 00:23:32.950
[S09] Oh, do we?

454
00:23:33.170 --> 00:23:34.690
[S09] All right, get that in there now.

455
00:23:34.819 --> 00:23:36.319
[S09] And I'm going to show you a lariat.

456
00:23:38.319 --> 00:23:40.390
[S09] There, going to show you a lariat.

457
00:25:00.740 --> 00:25:04.259
[S11] So here is a line of endeavor for the human intellect.

458
00:25:04.289 --> 00:25:08.230
[S11] When it attacks a problem, it must make things simple first.

459
00:25:08.259 --> 00:25:09.359
[S12] Progressively.

460
00:25:09.390 --> 00:25:15.269
[S11] As Einstein said, "In the beginning, things must be made as simple as possible."

461
00:25:15.299 --> 00:25:16.269
[S11] Einstein.

462
00:25:16.299 --> 00:25:23.539
[S11] Now, if then we understand what these things do, I have a problem.

463
00:25:24.539 --> 00:25:25.960
[S11] Here is a football.

464
00:25:25.990 --> 00:25:28.410
[S11] And that's a Yankee kind, isn't it?

465
00:25:28.440 --> 00:25:32.140
[S11] We might imagine this football to be an ellipsoid of revolution.

466
00:25:32.170 --> 00:25:35.069
[S11] You know, an ellipse which is spun on its long axis.

467
00:25:35.099 --> 00:25:38.349
[S11] So when I spin this football like this,

468
00:25:38.380 --> 00:25:42.910
[S11] it is spinning about an axis for which the moment of inertia is so much.

469
00:25:42.940 --> 00:25:45.269
[S11] Now watch what the football does.

470
00:25:45.299 --> 00:25:46.549
[S11] Watch it.

471
00:25:46.579 --> 00:25:48.549
[S11] Oh, what do you think it'll do?

472
00:25:48.579 --> 00:25:50.230
[S12] And will it behave the same as the lariat?

473
00:25:50.259 --> 00:25:51.029
[S11] Yeah.

474
00:25:51.059 --> 00:25:53.190
[S11] Well, notice, this is the long axis now.

475
00:25:53.220 --> 00:25:53.750
[S12] Yes.

476
00:25:53.779 --> 00:25:54.900
[S11] Watch it.

477
00:25:57.539 --> 00:25:59.750
[S11] Oh, I'll have to do it again.

478
00:25:59.779 --> 00:26:00.630
[S11] Oh, no.

479
00:26:00.660 --> 00:26:02.109
[S11] Uh-oh.

480
00:26:02.140 --> 00:26:02.750
[S12] Can I have it?

481
00:26:02.779 --> 00:26:03.029
[S12] Here we are.

482
00:26:03.059 --> 00:26:03.750
[S11] Yes.

483
00:26:03.779 --> 00:26:08.650
[S11] Notice, notice, one loses his professional dignity in these experiments.

484
00:26:10.460 --> 00:26:11.750
[S11] There it is, there it is.

485
00:26:11.779 --> 00:26:13.029
[S11] It came up on end.

486
00:26:13.059 --> 00:26:16.460
[S11] Notice, I'm so delighted with this that I'm going to do it again.

487
00:26:17.500 --> 00:26:19.190
[S11] There it is, there it is.

488
00:26:19.220 --> 00:26:20.750
[S11] Do you see what it did?

489
00:26:20.779 --> 00:26:23.190
[S11] First, it was rotating about that axis.

490
00:26:23.220 --> 00:26:25.789
[S11] Then it took rotation about that axis.

491
00:26:25.819 --> 00:26:29.190
[S11] And this introduces a very serious problem

492
00:26:29.220 --> 00:26:32.670
[S11] for the physicist, the mathematician, and the student of nature.

493
00:26:32.700 --> 00:26:35.190
[S11] Why does it do this?

494
00:26:35.220 --> 00:26:39.309
[S11] Is it violating the theorem which I spoke of before?

495
00:26:39.339 --> 00:26:40.990
[S11] Or is it not?

496
00:26:41.019 --> 00:26:42.549
[S11] Suggestion, hint.

497
00:26:42.579 --> 00:26:45.259
[S11] See, I hint only a very little bit.

498
00:26:46.259 --> 00:26:50.869
[S11] Man, man must agree with the principles of nature.

499
00:26:50.900 --> 00:26:53.230
[S11] Hence, there is no violation here.

500
00:26:53.259 --> 00:26:56.549
[S11] But where the dilemma lies, I leave for the observer.

501
00:26:56.579 --> 00:26:57.539
[S11] Watch it.

502
00:26:58.339 --> 00:26:58.869
[S11] There it is again.

503
00:26:58.900 --> 00:26:59.430
[S12] Up again.

504
00:26:59.460 --> 00:26:59.829
[S11] Now.

505
00:26:59.859 --> 00:27:01.990
[S12] Professor, you have about three minutes, two minutes left, I think.

506
00:27:02.019 --> 00:27:02.549
[S11] Two minutes.

507
00:27:02.579 --> 00:27:03.900
[S11] Good, two minutes.

508
00:27:04.619 --> 00:27:07.069
[S11] We will entitle this adventure,

509
00:27:07.099 --> 00:27:09.950
[S11] "The Case of the Notched Stick."

510
00:27:09.980 --> 00:27:11.390
[S11] Let's get over here

511
00:27:11.420 --> 00:27:16.109
[S11] and see what an enjoyable exercise we can have with a stick.

512
00:27:16.140 --> 00:27:19.269
[S11] Now, let me see how it looks on camera.

513
00:27:19.299 --> 00:27:20.259
[S11] Good.

514
00:27:20.740 --> 00:27:23.750
[S11] I have a stick, rectangular cross-section,

515
00:27:23.779 --> 00:27:25.750
[S11] which I have notched with a file.

516
00:27:25.779 --> 00:27:27.549
[S11] You see, you've just notched.

517
00:27:27.579 --> 00:27:30.750
[S11] And on the end of the stick, I have put a little propeller,

518
00:27:30.779 --> 00:27:33.430
[S11] fixed with a little nail in the middle.

519
00:27:33.460 --> 00:27:34.069
[S11] You see, how did?

520
00:27:34.099 --> 00:27:35.140
[S12] Yes.

521
00:27:35.180 --> 00:27:38.029
[S11] Now, what am I going to do with this?

522
00:27:38.049 --> 00:27:40.390
[S11] I'm going to lay this on the edge of the table,

523
00:27:40.420 --> 00:27:43.789
[S11] and with another stick, which I call the stroker,

524
00:27:43.819 --> 00:27:49.230
[S11] I am going to make that propeller behave in a manner unbelievable.

525
00:27:49.259 --> 00:27:50.349
[S11] Watch.

526
00:27:50.380 --> 00:27:51.259
[S11] Watch.

527
00:27:54.660 --> 00:27:56.549
[S11] What do you see that propeller doing?

528
00:27:56.579 --> 00:27:57.789
[S12] Spinning, I can't see much direction.

529
00:27:57.819 --> 00:27:59.539
[S11] Oh, watch it now, watch it.

530
00:28:01.779 --> 00:28:03.390
[S12] Kicked over to the spinning.

531
00:28:03.420 --> 00:28:04.430
[S11] Right, watch it now.

532
00:28:06.380 --> 00:28:06.829
[S11] Now the other way.

533
00:28:06.859 --> 00:28:07.430
[S12] Spinning clockwise.

534
00:28:07.460 --> 00:28:08.069
[S11] Right.

535
00:28:08.099 --> 00:28:11.349
[S11] Do you see that I can make this propeller go counterclockwise

536
00:28:11.380 --> 00:28:12.869
[S11] or clockwise as I please?

537
00:28:12.900 --> 00:28:13.660
[S11] Watch it.

538
00:28:16.299 --> 00:28:17.900
[S11] That's this way for me.

539
00:28:20.259 --> 00:28:22.190
[S11] That's this way for me.

540
00:28:22.220 --> 00:28:23.750
[S11] Is not this a delight?

541
00:28:23.779 --> 00:28:25.430
[S11] Now, this is a child's toy,

542
00:28:25.460 --> 00:28:29.269
[S11] which every boy and girl in Australia who sees this program can make.

543
00:28:29.299 --> 00:28:29.670
[S11] See?
